00:00:34.946 --> 00:00:36.976
- So you went to Harvard
and you dropped out.

00:00:36.976 --> 00:00:39.235
Have you ever thought how
your life could be better off

00:00:39.235 --> 00:00:41.888
if you had gotten your Harvard degree?

00:00:41.888 --> 00:00:44.763
- Well I'm a weird dropout
because I take college

00:00:44.763 --> 00:00:49.050
courses all the time, I love
learning company courses

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.279
and things, so I love being a student

00:00:52.279 --> 00:00:56.177
and there were smart people
around and they fed you

00:00:56.177 --> 00:00:58.858
and they gave you these
nice grades that made you

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:03.025
feel smart, so I feel it was
unfortunate that I didn't

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.307
get to stay there, but I don't
think I missed any knowledge

00:01:08.307 --> 00:01:10.721
because whatever I needed to learn,

00:01:10.721 --> 00:01:13.366
I was still in a learning mode.

00:01:13.366 --> 00:01:16.585
- If you could talk to your younger self,

00:01:16.585 --> 00:01:20.842
knowing what you know, and
those days when you thought,

00:01:20.842 --> 00:01:23.118
"Maybe it's not going to happen."

00:01:23.118 --> 00:01:24.690
What would you tell yourself?

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.728
- Play it cool.

00:01:26.728 --> 00:01:30.811
I'd say, I'd say, "Play
it cool and learn as much

00:01:33.697 --> 00:01:38.182
"as you can because everything
you learn, you're going to need.

00:01:38.182 --> 00:01:40.853
"You won't have enough time to learn.

00:01:40.853 --> 00:01:43.088
"Everything is an education."

00:01:43.088 --> 00:01:45.546
- You should learn something
from each and every single

00:01:45.546 --> 00:01:48.489
round you play in, every
single round you should learn

00:01:48.489 --> 00:01:52.082
something, whether it's one
thing, it might be 20 things.

00:01:52.082 --> 00:01:54.012
Learn something for each and every round.

00:01:54.012 --> 00:01:55.821
And I've done that, every
round that I've played,

00:01:55.821 --> 00:01:58.620
I've come back home,
I've critiqued my round,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.926
I've looked at it from the
mental and mechanical side,

00:02:01.926 --> 00:02:03.860
what went wrong, what went right,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.825
and apply it to the next day.

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:09.693
- What is it that makes
you real special champion?

00:02:09.693 --> 00:02:11.993
- Well, I just, I never,
well for one, I never quit

00:02:11.993 --> 00:02:14.457
and never stopped skating, I
mean, I never took a hiatus

00:02:14.457 --> 00:02:17.028
of, say, five years and came
like, "This seems hot now"

00:02:17.028 --> 00:02:18.478
and then came back into it.

00:02:18.478 --> 00:02:22.645
So I have that consistency
but I think that for me,

00:02:24.808 --> 00:02:27.412
I really liked learning new techniques.

00:02:27.412 --> 00:02:31.287
And a lot of people, if
they're seeing this one side

00:02:31.287 --> 00:02:34.373
of skating or this one set of tricks

00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:36.166
and don't want to branch
out because they're afraid

00:02:36.166 --> 00:02:38.653
they won't look cool doing it or whatever,

00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:41.561
I always wanted to try
the different techniques,

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.299
even if I stumble on them.

00:02:43.299 --> 00:02:46.332
I knew that in the end,
it would help me be more

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.315
well-rounded and I think
that's probably what it is,

00:02:48.315 --> 00:02:51.586
is that I just wanted to
try it and learn it all.

00:02:51.586 --> 00:02:54.669
- Learning, learning uses creativity.

00:02:56.516 --> 00:02:58.933
Creativity leads to thinking.

00:03:00.992 --> 00:03:03.325
Thinking provides knowledge.

00:03:04.169 --> 00:03:06.851
Knowledge makes you great.

00:03:06.851 --> 00:03:10.234
- Did you have a sense that
Coldplay has reached a stage

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:12.897
where you just didn't
want it to be that band?

00:03:12.897 --> 00:03:14.108
Like you wanted something else?

00:03:14.108 --> 00:03:15.343
- No, we reached a stage where we thought,

00:03:15.343 --> 00:03:18.808
"We can't get much bigger so
we have to try and get better."

00:03:18.808 --> 00:03:20.000
- Yeah.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.419
- And it's that whole thing
of quantity or quality.

00:03:24.419 --> 00:03:28.586
We thought, "Well, we need to
practice a bit and improve"

00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:34.782
and I was feeling bad
because I'm sure there's some

00:03:34.782 --> 00:03:39.033
16-year-olds or kids who
say they like Coldplay

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:42.130
and then took some abuse
for it in the playground

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.223
and so I had a feeling
about 18 months ago,

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:48.390
I really would like to make
them proud by trying to improve.

00:03:51.816 --> 00:03:54.380
- I came aboard a slogan
and I tell it to my kids

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.547
all the time, I say, "There's
no losing, only learning,

00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:02.165
"there's on failure, only opportunities,

00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:05.093
"and there's no problems, only solutions."

00:04:05.093 --> 00:04:06.891
So to me, what failure
is, failure is the mother

00:04:06.891 --> 00:04:09.347
of all success, if it
wasn't for Michael Jordan

00:04:09.347 --> 00:04:11.465
getting cut from his
ninth-grade basketball team,

00:04:11.465 --> 00:04:13.395
he wouldn't have became Michael Jordan.

00:04:13.395 --> 00:04:16.585
You know, if it wasn't for, I
seen an article the other day

00:04:16.585 --> 00:04:18.576
where they were talking about
Oprah Winfrey and how she got

00:04:18.576 --> 00:04:21.844
fired because she wasn't
good for television.

00:04:21.844 --> 00:04:25.711
You know, you got people like
Walt Disney who got fired,

00:04:25.711 --> 00:04:27.254
if I'm not mistaken, from a newspaper,

00:04:27.254 --> 00:04:29.671
saying he had no imagination.

00:04:30.684 --> 00:04:33.988
(speaking Spanish)

00:04:33.988 --> 00:04:35.643
These are the same people
that I got to deal with,

00:04:35.643 --> 00:04:39.049
telling me what's not a hit record.

00:04:39.049 --> 00:04:41.670
- [Interviewer] So what do
you tell 'em, you tell 'em?

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.752
- You know, that all they can do is learn

00:04:43.752 --> 00:04:45.772
and come back bigger, better, stronger,

00:04:45.772 --> 00:04:48.181
'cause all it's going to do is
lead you in the right direction.

00:04:48.181 --> 00:04:50.249
So if you're always winning,
then you don't really

00:04:50.249 --> 00:04:53.447
understand what it is to win,
you got to take those losses,

00:04:53.447 --> 00:04:55.627
you got to take those hits,
it's got to be the valleys,

00:04:55.627 --> 00:04:59.048
the peaks, the ups, the
downs, in order for you to,

00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:01.678
when it does happen, you go, wow.

00:05:01.678 --> 00:05:03.150
(speaking Spanish)

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.492
This is what it's all
about, and not only that,

00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:07.231
it's never about making
it, guys, it's always about

00:05:07.231 --> 00:05:10.628
maintaining it, that's the toughest part.

00:05:10.628 --> 00:05:12.961
- What one quality do you think you've had

00:05:12.961 --> 00:05:16.344
that made you and gave
you and allowed you,

00:05:16.344 --> 00:05:20.095
pushed you, to achieve what you have?

00:05:20.095 --> 00:05:23.479
- I think my willingness to
learn, I'm pretty relentless

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:27.970
when it comes to that, I
certainly knew everything

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:30.219
about growing up on the
street and you know,

00:05:30.219 --> 00:05:32.822
what it took to survive on
the street corner in Dorcester

00:05:32.822 --> 00:05:35.094
but this is a whole new world to me

00:05:35.094 --> 00:05:39.147
so just being able to take
advantage of the things

00:05:39.147 --> 00:05:42.950
that I've seen and the things
that I've been exposed to now

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:44.590
with my career, has really been.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:46.903
- I continually learn from my mistakes

00:05:46.903 --> 00:05:48.846
and other people's mistakes.

00:05:48.846 --> 00:05:51.942
See, entrepreneurs think that,
hey, to create that billion

00:05:51.942 --> 00:05:53.888
dollar company, or hundred
million dollar company

00:05:53.888 --> 00:05:56.304
or even million dollar company,
you have to be really smart,

00:05:56.304 --> 00:06:00.945
and sure, brains is part of
it, luck is a huge component,

00:06:00.945 --> 00:06:04.150
but what I've learned over
the years is, everyone,

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.791
no matter how smart you are,
you're going to fail many times

00:06:06.791 --> 00:06:08.283
and you're going to make mistakes,

00:06:08.283 --> 00:06:09.650
just look at Mark Zuckerberg.

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:12.639
Facebook worked out,
his version of SnapChat

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:15.693
that was a copycat, did
not work out, right?

00:06:15.693 --> 00:06:18.680
So there's other areas
in which Facebook failed

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.193
but Facebook overall as
a company has done great.

00:06:21.193 --> 00:06:23.049
Just because you're smart
doesn't mean you're always

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:25.875
going to succeed with
everything you try or touch.

00:06:25.875 --> 00:06:30.057
But every time you do something,
you will learn new things,

00:06:30.057 --> 00:06:32.704
you'll make mistakes, and if
you learn from these mistakes

00:06:32.704 --> 00:06:35.888
and avoid making 'em over and over again,

00:06:35.888 --> 00:06:38.477
you'll actually increase
your odds of succeeding

00:06:38.477 --> 00:06:40.559
as time goes on, and
the best way to do this

00:06:40.559 --> 00:06:42.462
is not just to learn from your mistakes,

00:06:42.462 --> 00:06:45.522
but it's also to learn from
the mistakes of others.

00:06:45.522 --> 00:06:47.864
Rockefeller, Carnegie, all these people

00:06:47.864 --> 00:06:51.457
who were entrepreneurs years
ago, in the 1800s, 1900s,

00:06:51.457 --> 00:06:55.480
they themselves used business
principles and concepts

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.173
that are still applicable
in today's world.

00:06:58.173 --> 00:07:00.169
Sure, we have the internet,
but the same strategies

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:03.234
and concepts that they used
are still applicable, right?

00:07:03.234 --> 00:07:05.749
So learn from their mistakes,
learn what worked for them,

00:07:05.749 --> 00:07:08.247
what didn't work for
them, why, and bring that

00:07:08.247 --> 00:07:10.421
into what you're doing today.

00:07:10.421 --> 00:07:12.116
- As you look back over your
life, if there's just one

00:07:12.116 --> 00:07:16.659
thing that you could do
differently, what would it be?

00:07:16.659 --> 00:07:17.492
- Hmm.

00:07:18.848 --> 00:07:20.145
That's always a tough question,

00:07:20.145 --> 00:07:23.267
I get that question asked a lot.

00:07:23.267 --> 00:07:25.873
I'm not sure, you know, because
everything that happened

00:07:25.873 --> 00:07:29.456
in my life produced
me, even the negatives,

00:07:32.048 --> 00:07:34.107
even the things that I
might have failed in,

00:07:34.107 --> 00:07:36.934
the things I made mistakes
with, they were fantastic

00:07:36.934 --> 00:07:40.616
classrooms for me and I believe
the best thing in life to do

00:07:40.616 --> 00:07:43.725
is not to regret and try
to change what happened,

00:07:43.725 --> 00:07:45.475
but to interpret it properly,

00:07:45.475 --> 00:07:48.137
maximize it and use it
to serve other people

00:07:48.137 --> 00:07:51.285
and that's what I want to do with my past.

00:07:51.285 --> 00:07:52.572
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:07:52.572 --> 00:07:53.756
I hope you enjoyed.

00:07:53.756 --> 00:07:55.736
I'd love to know whose
message had the biggest impact

00:07:55.736 --> 00:07:58.098
on you, what was the clip that left you

00:07:58.098 --> 00:08:00.040
wanting to make an immediate impact,

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.121
and what are some of the
challenges that you have

00:08:02.121 --> 00:08:04.257
in staying motivated to keep learning?

00:08:04.257 --> 00:08:06.920
Leave that in the comments
below, super curious to find out,

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:08.641
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:08:08.641 --> 00:08:11.045
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Shlomo Awoke,

00:08:11.045 --> 00:08:12.903
thank you so much for
that Instagram picture

00:08:12.903 --> 00:08:15.843
and for picking up a copy
of my book, Your One Word,

00:08:15.843 --> 00:08:17.749
it really really really means a lot to me.

00:08:17.749 --> 00:08:20.083
So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you,

00:08:20.083 --> 00:08:22.437
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:08:22.437 --> 00:08:26.604
and whatever your one word is,
much love, I'll see you soon.

